macOS audio backends (M1 S1)
----------------------------

Capability/license spike result: a viable native system-audio path exists on
macOS 14.2+ and does NOT require a virtual loopback device. Two upstream paths
were checked against Apple's own documentation:

  1. ScreenCaptureKit audio. Apple's DocC data for
     `SCStreamConfiguration.capturesAudio` states "A Boolean value that indicates
     whether to capture audio", that streams do not capture audio by default,
     and an availability floor of macOS 13.0. The Rust binding
     `screencapturekit` is maintained by doom-fish
     (https://github.com/doom-fish/screencapturekit-rs); crates.io API records
     version 12.0.0 published 2026-11-21, checksum
     b5f891b0db00740f80bf876cfb83ae67c1484924be9cbf9d1b4aee87b1511108, license
     `MIT OR Apache-2.0`, rust-version 1.82. Its README documents system audio
     capture from macOS 13.0+ but microphone capture only from macOS 15.0+,
     which is above Buddy's committed macOS 14.2 floor, so this crate is NOT
     adopted. It is recorded here as evidence that a virtual device is unneeded.

  2. CoreAudio process taps. Apple's DocC metadata for
     `AudioHardwareCreateProcessTap` records `introducedAt: 14.2` for macOS.
     Apple's "Capturing system audio with Core Audio taps" sample
     (developer.apple.com/documentation/coreaudio/capturing-system-audio-with-core-audio-taps)
     says "ensure that you're using macOS 14.2 or later", creates a tap with
     `AudioHardwareCreateProcessTap` and reads it through a HAL aggregate device.
     It requires the `NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription` Info.plist key and the
     user's System Audio Recording permission, and explicitly needs no virtual
     audio device. Apple's CoreAudio framework ships with macOS; there is no
     third-party runtime licence to attribute for the framework itself.

Chosen binding: the upstream `coreaudio` crate, pinned to exactly 0.6.2 in
crates/buddy-capture/Cargo.toml with its `process-tap` feature. It is maintained
by Keegan Wilgermein (https://github.com/Keegan-Wilgermein/coreaudio). Sources
checked: the crates.io API record for `coreaudio` 0.6.2 (license `MIT`,
checksum 44f8d03f64a1e3a4fc093b793351ee901bceb52ee82a139072a808c2d4fec4bb,
published 2026-10-21), the docs.rs 0.6.2 page (MIT, edition 2024), the published
crate source Cargo.toml, LICENSE and README (which document `ProcessTap`,
`AppTapAggregateDevice`, `IOProc` and the macOS 14.2+ floor), and Apple's
process-tap and ScreenCaptureKit documentation. The crate taps all CoreAudio
processes for `TrackKind::System` and opens the default input device for
`TrackKind::Mic`. Tapping needs the System Audio Recording TCC grant; without
it the tap is created but delivers silence, so the desktop shell must add
`NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription` before a real-capture attempt can succeed.

